\name{cellucid_camera_path_playback_set}
\alias{cellucid_camera_path_playback_set}
\title{Set camera path playback}
\usage{
cellucid_camera_path_playback_set(
  viewer,
  autoplay,
  captions,
  easing,
  loop,
  position_interpolation,
  rotation_interpolation,
  speed
)
}
\arguments{
\item{viewer}{A viewer \code{\link{cellucid_view}()} or \code{\link{cellucid_serve}()} returned.}
\item{autoplay}{Start playing as soon as the path has two keyframes. \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{captions}{Show each named keyframe's label as a caption over the plot while the path plays (on by default; a keyframe still called "KF n" has none). \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{easing}{How the path starts and ends: from rest and to rest (ease-in-out), from rest (ease-in), to rest (ease-out), or moving (linear); every transition keeps its own time. One of \code{"linear"}, \code{"ease-in-out"}, \code{"ease-in"}, \code{"ease-out"}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{loop}{Play the path again from its start when it ends. \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{position_interpolation}{How positions between keyframes are drawn: catmull-rom is the smooth one (no jolt or overshoot at a keyframe; a keyframe repeated is a hold). One of \code{"catmull-rom"}, \code{"linear"}, \code{"bezier"}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{rotation_interpolation}{How the view direction turns between keyframes. One of \code{"slerp"}, \code{"linear"}. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{speed}{Speed of automatic transitions, 1 (very slow) to 100 (very fast). A whole number (at least 1, at most 100). Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
}
\value{
A \code{cellucid_action_request}, at once and invisibly: the action is sent now, or when the viewer's channel opens.
\code{\link{cellucid_action_result}(request)} returns its outcome (what it did, as names, counts and ids), or \code{NULL} when it had nothing to report; \code{request$step} is the history step it made.
}
\description{
Set how the camera path plays: interpolation, easing, speed of automatic transitions, loop, autoplay and captions.
}
\details{
The viewer's action \code{camera-path.playback.set} (Camera path). It changes what the viewer shows: one step of the viewer's history, labeled as R's, undone like any other. The state it can change: \code{camera-path}.
}
\seealso{
\code{\link{cellucid_act}()} sends any action by its type, and \code{\link{cellucid_action_result}()}
collects its answer.
}
\examples{
if (interactive() && requireNamespace("SeuratObject", quietly = TRUE)) {
  data("pbmc_small", package = "SeuratObject")
  viewer <- cellucid_view(SeuratObject::UpdateSeuratObject(pbmc_small))
  request <- cellucid_camera_path_playback_set(viewer)
  print(request)
  # cellucid_action_result(request) waits for its outcome.
  cellucid_stop(viewer)
}
}
